    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Solve $x^3+3x^2-2x-4=0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=-1,-1\\pm\\sqrt5$.",
      "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^3+3x^2-2x-4$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-1)&=-1+3+2-4\\\\P(-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $x+1$ is a factor. Polynomial division gives\n$$P(x)=(x+1)(x^2+2x-4).$$\nHence $x=-1$ or\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x-4&=0\\\\(x+1)^2&=5\\\\x+1&=\\pm\\sqrt5\\\\x&=-1\\pm\\sqrt5.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
